---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import VideoEmbed from '../components/VideoEmbed.astro';
import { currentPartners as p } from '../lib/partners';

// Copy: EP's own edit pass, July 2026 — no longer the action-plan draft.
// Huawei is named with permission on all three levels: naming them as a
// client, describing the nature of the work, and citing the campaign's
// reach. EP is running the final wording past them before this ships.
// KY figures (Secretary General, €500k budget) match the profile page.
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Partners"
  lang="en"
  description="Partnership, speaking and advisory work with Esa-Pekka Mattila, T54 wheelchair racer, through LA28. What a partnership involves and what a season costs."
>
  <section class="partners-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Partners</p>
      <h1>Partnership,<br />speaking,<br />advisory<span class="accent-text">.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">
        I am open to partners through <a href="https://la28.org/en.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LA28</a>. This page sets out what
        that means in practice and what it costs, so a conversation can
        start from something concrete.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="narrative" id="partnership">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head reveal"><span class="idx mono">01</span><h2>Partnership</h2></div>
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <p>
          A season at world level costs around €20,000 before living
          costs: racing chairs, training camps, flights, physiotherapy,
          competition entry fees, equipment that wears out and gets
          replaced, and other costs such as coaching and expert help. My
          competitive plan runs from the World Championships in Tashkent
          in June 2027 through the European Championships in 2028, with
          Los Angeles 2028 as the last highlight of my career.
        </p>
        <p>
          My own channels are small and I won't pretend otherwise. They
          are open to a partner as well, they are simply not where the
          reach comes from. What I bring is the material: twenty years
          of competing at world level, a governance career running
          alongside it, and a track record of saying true things on
          camera. What a partner brings is distribution. When Huawei
          filmed the work we did together on wearable technology, Wheels
          of Change reached over 13 million views, <a
          href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zKV9IRRmU3U"
          target="_blank" rel="noopener">7.7 million on YouTube</a> and
          <a
          href="https://www.facebook.com/huawei/videos/wheels-of-change/1425655212392731/"
          target="_blank" rel="noopener">5.7 million on Facebook</a>, on
          their channels rather than mine. That is the division of
          labour I am proposing, and it has worked once already.
        </p>
      </div>
      {/* The paragraph above makes a claim about reach; the film is the
          evidence for it, so it sits directly under that paragraph rather
          than at the end of the section. Huawei's channel, Huawei's film —
          this embeds it, it does not rehost it. */}
      <div class="wheels reveal">
        <VideoEmbed
          id="zKV9IRRmU3U"
          title="Wheels of Change — Huawei, featuring Esa-Pekka Mattila"
          label="Wheels of Change"
          meta="Huawei · 13M+ views · 7.7M on YouTube"
          play="Play"
          fallback="Watch Wheels of Change on YouTube"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <p>
          Terms are defined case by case. I am looking for a small
          number of meaningful partners, not a shirt full of logos.
        </p>
        {/* This lands immediately after "not a shirt full of logos", which is
            the point of putting it here: the naming becomes the evidence for
            the claim directly above it rather than a separate boast. One full
            paragraph of separation from the Huawei reach figures, so the two
            are not read as one list of sponsors.

            The English carries a gloss the Finnish does not — a Finnish
            reader knows what Apteekki-tuotesarja is, a foreign one would read
            it as a corner shop rather than the pharmacies' national trade
            brand. Deliberate divergence, not a missing translation. */}
        <p>
          Current partners: <a href={p.apteekki.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{p.apteekki.name}</a>,
          the Finnish pharmacies' own product range, and <a href={p.linjaaho.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{p.linjaaho.name}</a> in
          Espoo. Both cover the parts of a season that never appear in a
          results list.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="narrative" id="speaking">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head reveal"><span class="idx mono">02</span><h2>Speaking</h2></div>
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <p>
          Keynotes, panels and workshops, in Finnish and English.
        </p>
      </div>
      {/* Two named offers rather than three merged paragraphs. The copy is
          almost entirely what was already here — naming it separates the
          thing only EP can sell from the thing a buyer has many options
          for, and gives each one somewhere to carry its own evidence. */}
      <div class="offer reveal">
        <h3>Governance and athlete representation</h3>
        <p>
          Athlete representation where it actually matters: selection
          processes, funding disagreements, and balancing stakeholders
          who want different things. A federation cannot also be the
          athletes' representative in those rooms, however it is framed.
          Disability inclusion approached the same way. In both, naming
          the problem is the easy half; the work is building what
          resolves it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="offer reveal">
        <h3>Sport to business</h3>
        <p>
          Decision-making under constraint, and what elite sport systems
          can teach everyday working life and business.
        </p>
        {/* Frey's testimonial is about coaching leadership, shared goals and
            mental resilience, so it belongs to this offer and not to the
            governance one above. Cleared for publication by Juha Frey on
            2.8.2026 (rule 29). The Finnish is his original and lives on the
            FI page; this is a translation of it. */}
        <figure class="quote">
          <blockquote>
            <p>
              Esa-Pekka's talk at Netprofile's internal training event was
              excellent. In it he built a superb bridge between sport and
              the business world. He shared concrete examples from his
              athletic career — situations where leadership, determination,
              the importance of recovery and the capacity to withstand
              pressure proved decisive. He reflected those experiences
              skilfully against the daily reality of organisations and the
              challenges of business. The talk was practical, relatable and
              energising. It prompted discussion and gave us concrete means
              of applying approaches familiar from sport — coaching
              leadership, clarifying a shared goal, strengthening mental
              resilience — to developing an organisation.
            </p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption class="mono">
            Juha Frey, CEO, Netprofile
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="narrative" id="advisory">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head reveal"><span class="idx mono">03</span><h2>Advisory</h2></div>
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <p>
          Advisory work in wheelchair and assistive technology, such as
          with Huawei, taking a product feature from athlete feedback to
          shipped release. Also accessibility and governance questions,
          where lived experience, board experience and a business-law
          background merge. Earlier I was Secretary General of KY at
          Aalto University, with an annual budget over €500,000, which
          is where the operational understanding comes from.
        </p>
        <p>
          Always open, from a sparring session to a clearly defined
          project.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="partners-cta">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="rule-accent"></div>
      <p class="cta-line">Interested?</p>
      <a href="mailto:ep@esapekkamattila.com" class="cta-mail">ep@esapekkamattila.com</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .partners-hero {
    padding-top: clamp(140px, 20vh, 200px);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }
  .partners-hero h1 {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
  }
  .accent-text {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .partners-hero .lead {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    font-size: var(--text-lead);
    line-height: 1.3;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    max-width: 32rem;
  }

  .narrative {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    /* Clears the fixed nav when arriving via /partners#speaking */
    scroll-margin-top: 90px;
  }
  .narrative .prose p {
    font-size: clamp(1.0625rem, 1.5vw, 1.1875rem);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  /* Matches .narrative .prose p's measure so the film sits in the same
     column as the paragraph it evidences. The section's prose is split
     either side of it, so the block carries its own vertical rhythm. */
  .wheels {
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin-block: var(--space-4);
  }
  .narrative .prose p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  /* A named offer inside section 02. Same measure as the prose so the two
     read as one column, with the heading carrying the separation rather
     than a rule or a box. */
  .offer {
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }
  .offer h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-h3);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
  }
  .offer p {
    font-size: clamp(1.0625rem, 1.5vw, 1.1875rem);
  }
  /* The accent bar does the attribution's work visually, so the quote does
     not need italics or quote marks to read as someone else's voice. */
  .quote {
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    padding-left: var(--space-2);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
  }
  .quote blockquote {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .quote blockquote p {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: var(--line-body);
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .quote figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .sec-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 16px;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }
  .sec-head .idx {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
  }

  .partners-cta {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .cta-line {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--text-h2);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: var(--line-tight);
  }
  .cta-mail {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }
</style>
